    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d180">

                <pb facs="0330" n="314" sameAs="frus:frus1950v02/pg314"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">330.1/7–3150: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Douglas</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-07-31T18:00:00+01:00">July 31, 1950—6 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>659. Embtel 634. Embassy officer learned from Foreign Office that before
                    instructions sent to Franks giving British view of proposed resolution to
                    establish fact finding observation commission matter was discussed with Bevin.
                    He was unwilling to take decision without consulting Prime Minister.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d180-f1"/> Matter was, therefore, laid before Prime Minister
                    who decided that UK position could not be determined without Cabinet
                    decision.</p>
                <p>As British position already outlined to Hickerson by Franks it is not reported
                    here. Following observations, however, of possible interest: View at working
                    level Foreign Office is very strong that resolution is <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >ultra vires</hi> and contrary to undertaking at San Francisco.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d180-f2"/> Passage of such resolution in absence of Soviet delegate,
                    it is felt, would give Russians excellent propaganda material. British are <pb
                        facs="0331" n="315" xml:id="pg315"/> therefore pleased with Hickerson’s
                    assurance that resolutions will not be pressed to vote on July 31.</p>
                <p> UN Department is busily devising instructions to Jebb on various courses of
                    action to meet hypothetical Russian moves in SC August 1.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Douglas</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d180-f1" n="1" xml:id="d180-f1-content">
                <p>Clement Attlee.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d180-f2" n="2" xml:id="d180-f2-content">
                <p>A reference to the Four-Power Statement of June 7, 1945, on voting procedure in
                    the Security Council.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
